posts.body:
I'm currently trying to convert the tutorial
Letting The Player Choose Their Character
into a script as it's an unreadable mess and a pain to update as events only, however, I've ran into the issue of not knowing how to handle recursive choice selections. I saw the following threads
Question about Nested Choices via JS
and
Message window doesn't show when called more than once
but the first one isn't actually answered and the second one isn't script only. I'm not really sure on how to handle this. I'm
sure
there's a more complex character creation plugin, but I see no point in using one for something this simple.
I also saw
this
but it doesn't go into this either.
The code as-is:
JavaScript:
const characters = [
  { name: 'Kasey', class: 2, event_id: 26, image_id: 5, battle_image_id: "Actor1_5" },
  { name: 'Eliot', class: 3, event_id: 27, image_id: 6, battle_image_id: "Actor1_6" },
  { name: 'Roza', class: 3, event_id: 28, image_id: 7, battle_image_id: "Actor1_7" }
];

$gameMessage.add("Welcome Hero! It's time to face your destiny.\nNow, who are you?");
$gameMessage.setChoices(['Kasey', 'Eliot', 'Roza'], 1, -1);
$gameMessage.setChoiceCallback(index => {
  character = characters[index]
  $gameMap.event(character.event_id).moveStraight(2);

  // TODO: Show selected character's actor image. Part of the Yes/No choice selection process

  // TODO: Move this behind a separate yes/no choice selection
  // If no, the character event_id moves back once and goes back to the character selection menu
  $gameParty.members()[0].setName(character.name)
  $gameParty.members()[0].changeClass(2, true);
  $gameSwitches.setValue(2, true);

  // Plays animation
  const char = this.character(this._characterId = -1);
  $gameTemp.requestAnimation([char], 117);
  this.setWaitMode('animation');

  // Changes the player's character
  var actor = $gameParty.members()[0];
  actor.setCharacterImage('Actor1', character.image_id);
  actor.setFaceImage('Actor1', character.image_id);
  actor.setBattlerImage(character.battle_image_id);
  $gamePlayer.refresh();
  // TODO: Have the event send a message saying the character was selected
});

this.setWaitMode('message');

Oh boy. I hope you're not planning any fancy cutscenes for your game.
Trying to reproduce event commands with script calls is less straightforward, readable, maintainable, efficient, and concise. It's also more likely to break plugins. What I'm saying is: it's possible, but generally not the best idea.
You're having trouble because you're trying to use the event interpreter's timings (e.g. "do this after a choice has been selected") while largely avoiding use of the interpreter. Normally the interpreter works with a list of command objects, e.g. the core Show Choices callback simply jumps to the corresponding branch in the command list, and continues processing commands from there. Similarly, you'd need external structure to "go back to the character selection menu". Why reinvent the wheel?
If you're using this in a Script command in an event, you can just put the follow-up in another Script command after the first, e.g.
JavaScript:
// $gameMessage stuff...
$gameMessage.setChoiceCallback(index => {
  // store selected index in variable ID 123 for later reference
  $gameVariables.setValue(123, index);
});
JavaScript:
// Retrieve stored index.
const index = $gameVariables.value(123);
// more stuff~
The
this.setWaitMode("message")
line should prevent the interpreter from processing any further commands until the message is dismissed, which in this case involves selecting a choice.
I can't think of a good reason for it, but if you really
needed
to replace a series of event commands with scripting, the best approach might be to:
Build the list of new commands to insert;
(Deep-)clone the current command list;
Splice the new commands in at the current index; then
Assign the modified clone as the new command list.
Step 1 would involve writing commands in their p-code as detailed on the "Event Code" sheet of the Script Call Reference, a sister document to the Script Call List that you linked:
RPG Maker MZ Data and Script Call Reference
RPG Maker MZ Data and Script Call Reference  A collection of data and script calls for certain functions of RPG Maker MZ.   Supplementary Material:  Most of the script calls found in RPG Maker MV also works. If you want samples and notes to compare side by side. Please visit this topic...
forums.rpgmakerweb.com
This p-code can also be seen in a project's map files, e.g.
data/Map001.json
. There's an example on the Script Call List: search for
Insert Event Commands
(currently row 579).
